hwart a  military engagement basically what he's  saying is that the ruling party in the  tigray region  so basically his opposition party or one  of his opposition parties  has crossed a red line after months of  political tensions  and that he now has to intervene to  safeguard the country's stability  although many are saying that this is a  very dangerous move  now can you help us understand a bit  more here  what has led to the escalated situation  between tigray state  and the federal government  tensions have been growing between the  tigray people's liberation front or tplf  so basically the ruling party in the  tiguay region  and prime minister abi ahmed ever since  abi ahmed came to power in 2018. the  tegran party had  a huge political power for decades  before abi who is a from the oromia  region  was appointed prime minister so for the  past two years tigrayans had been  signed sidelined from the political  scene and its officials are feeling that  they are being  unfairly targeted and scapegoated for  the country's problems last year they  also refused to join abby's  new coalition party the prosperity party  and they opposed the government's  decision  to postpone elections due to the  coven-19 pandemic so this  has led these other tensions have led to  this escalation of which  we still um know little about okay  now uh briefly on this one how are  ordinary ethiopians reacting  to the increase in violence  as i said it's impossible to communicate  with anyone in tigre at the moment  and i would say people are mostly  worried worried because because they  cannot  talk to their relatives they're worried  about a possible armed conflict  they're asking themselves is there going  to be a civil war  um and yeah so we're seeing a lot of  people calling for authorities to work  towards de-escalation and conflict  resolution  maria get nicolescu dw correspondent in  ethiopia thank you for your time
 

